sortnetopt-decomp-v3 — decomposition machinery: shell counters, the shared
bound oracle, and prefix-job identity

Applies on top of the nine-patch v3 stack, in this order:

    sortnetopt-macos-proc.patch
    sortnetopt-instrumentation-v3.patch
    sortnetopt-online-subsumption-v3.patch
    sortnetopt-perf-v3.patch
    sortnetopt-tier1-v3.patch
    sortnetopt-tier2a-v3.patch
    sortnetopt-tier2b-v3.patch
    sortnetopt-limits-v3.patch
    sortnetopt-zm-v3.patch
    sortnetopt-decomp-v3.patch      <- this one

Base pin: 0b5d09c47446096f9e3a0812b35afc72b7f2a718.
Nothing in checker/ is touched. The frozen snocheck is never rebuilt.

This patch implements Steps 1 and 2 of the composed plan in
docs/lowmem-endgame-assessment.md §11, plus the engine-side identity hook the
per-(class x prefix) job decomposition needs. It adds no search heuristic, no
pruning rule and no bound. Every feature is env- or subcommand-gated and inert
by default.


================================================================================
PART 1 — SHELL INSTRUMENTATION (§11 Step 1 items 1 and 3)
================================================================================

Gate: SORTNETOPT_SHELL_STATS=1 (default off; when off, no popcount is computed,
no allocation happens, and the run is bit-identical to the nine-patch stack).

A state's *shell* is the pair (width, popcount) — the channel count of the
sub-problem and |X|, the number of set bits of the packed key. Because
packed_len_for_channels(k) = 2^(k-3) bytes exactly, with no padding bits at any
k >= 3, popcount(packed) == |X| exactly.

§7.1 of the assessment establishes, by exhaustive BFS at n <= 5 and random DP
walks at n <= 10, that (width, |X|) is a topological quasi-order on the DP DAG:
every edge decreases it, except for a measured 4-7% of comparator edges after
channel pruning that stay inside the same shell. Same-shell edges are the one
open risk in the shell-bucketed external memo design, because they force an inner
fixpoint whose depth is the number of sub-passes a k-pass sorted merge needs.
The assessment could only measure that depth along random walks (max 5 in ~9,700
edges) and named a counter as "the cheapest possible instrumentation ask in this
document". This is that counter, measured on the real DAG.

Counters added:
    shell_stats_enabled          config echo
    succ_edges_examined          denominator
    succ_same_shell              child (width, popcount) == parent's
    succ_width_drop              child width < parent width
    succ_smaller_same_width      same width, smaller popcount
    succ_shell_violation         MUST BE 0 — the Shell Lemma's machine check
    same_shell_chain_max         longest run of consecutive same-shell edges
    same_shell_chain_total       for a mean
    same_shell_chain_starts

instrument.json gains a "shell_census" object mapping "width,popcount" -> live
count (zeros omitted), plus the summary columns of assessment §7.2
(shells_nonzero, largest_shell, largest_shell_frac, top16_frac, top64_frac), so
a run reproduces shells.py for free instead of requiring a dump.

filter_exact_hits, the third Step-1 ask, already landed in the zm patch.


================================================================================
PART 2 — THE SHARED BOUND ORACLE (§11 Step 2, §6.3)
================================================================================

Gate: SORTNETOPT_BOUND_SEED=<path to a checkpoint.bin> (default off, inert).

A read-only seed-import path *beside* the existing checkpoint resume. It loads a
prior run's StateMap as {(width, packed key) -> bounds} seeds and merges them
into this run's StateMap. It is deliberately NOT the resume path:
SORTNETOPT_CHECKPOINT_RESUME still refuses any file whose identity string
differs (exit 3, no override), and that refusal is correct and unchanged. The
seed path accepts foreign identities on purpose, and the rest of this section is
the argument for why that is sound.

--- Soundness ---------------------------------------------------------------

State.bounds is, by construction, a closed interval on the minimum number of
comparators required to sort every vector of the output set. It is a property of
the SET. It does not depend on the root, the prefix, the class, or the channel
count n of the run that produced it.

The memo key is (width w, packed bitmap over the 2^w Boolean vectors). Because
packed_len_for_channels(w) = 2^(w-3) bytes exactly with no padding bits, the key
is a faithful, width-tagged encoding of the set and carries no residue of the
producing run. Therefore an entry stored at width w by an n'-channel run is a
statement about exactly the same mathematical object as an entry stored at
width w by an n-channel run.

BE PRECISE ABOUT WHAT IS IMPORTED:
  * bounds only, and only for entries with 3 <= w <= min(this run's channels,
    this build's MAX_CHANNELS). Wider entries are skipped and counted.
  * huffman_bounds are NOT imported. They are an internal headroom memo tied to
    the producing run's Huffman recursion, not a property of the set.
  * the on-line subsumption INDEX section of the checkpoint is not read at all.
  * the merge is
        lower := max(lower_existing, lower_imported)
        upper := min(upper_existing, upper_imported)
    which is sound because both are valid bounds on the same quantity, and
    monotone, which is exactly what improve() already requires
    (docs/sortnetopt-internals.md §3.6 invariants 1-2: the reader-visible lower
    bound never decreases).
  * a merge that would empty the interval is a CONTRADICTION and cannot happen
    if both runs are correct. It bumps seed_contradictions, logs both intervals
    and the key, and aborts with exit code 4. It is a corruption detector, not a
    recovery path.

The measurement in assessment §6.1 — level-4 memo tables of a full n=9 run and a
full n=10 run agreeing as sets of (width, packed key) pairs at Jaccard 0.9936,
with widths 3, 4, 5 and 9 bit-identical, and 99.9163% of the 207,777 shared keys
carrying the identical stored lower bound — is what justifies *expecting the
reuse to be large*. It is NOT what makes the import sound; soundness is the
definitional argument above, which holds at equal width for any pair of n. The
174 value disagreements are weakenings, not contradictions (a run stops improving
a node once the root's race is settled), which is why max/min is the right merge
and why no disagreement can be a soundness event.

--- The certificate obligation, and why this design discharges it ------------

assessment §6.3 item 3 warns that imported bounds have no in-run justification
and would strand GenProof::prove_all (internals §3.6 invariant 4). This design
avoids that, as follows.

The certificate is not generated from the search's stored bound VALUES. The
pipeline is search -> dump_states -> prune-all -> gen-proof, and dump_states
writes group_{w}_{b}.bin files containing packed keys ONLY — no State. gen-proof
re-derives every justification from the dumped collection of SETS. So the only
way seeding can break the certificate is by causing the search to omit a state
the proof needs.

Seed import therefore inserts the seed entries INTO the StateMap (with merged
bounds), not into a side table. dump_states drains the whole StateMap, so every
seeded state is dumped. The seed came from a run whose own dumped collection was
self-sufficient (it produced an accepted certificate); the union of two
self-sufficient collections is self-sufficient, because each state's
justification chain lies inside its own origin collection and the union preserves
membership. The obligation is discharged by construction — and it is checked
empirically by the validation gate, which is what actually decides it.

Seed entries are inserted by a dedicated StateMap::seed_state that mirrors
restore_state's discipline: the key is rebuilt as a PVec so the FxHasher shard is
computed identically to get/set (which is why the shard count is not part of any
identity and seeds are portable across machines and core counts); it bypasses
set() entirely (no index insert, no eviction) and does not inflate
set_calls/set_inserts.

The seed file is validated exactly as a checkpoint is — magic, format version,
header hash, trailer magic, and the FULL payload SHA-256 — with the single
deliberate exception of the identity-hash equality check. The seed's identity
string and its SHA-256 are read, logged, and recorded in instrument.json, so
every seeded run states on the record what it was seeded from.

Counters: seed_enabled, seed_entries_read, seed_entries_imported,
seed_entries_new, seed_entries_merged_stronger, seed_skipped_width,
seed_contradictions (must be 0), seed_import_ns; plus a "seed" object in
instrument.json carrying the path, the file hash and the per-width counts.

No new writer is needed to produce a seed: a final checkpoint already IS this
table. SORTNETOPT_CHECKPOINT_DIR=<d> with SORTNETOPT_CHECKPOINT_INTERVAL_SECS=0
writes <d>/checkpoint.bin, and that is the seed.


================================================================================
PART 3 — PREFIX-JOB IDENTITY (canon-key subcommand)
================================================================================

The engine already accepts a comparator prefix (-p/--prefix, flat i,j pairs),
which is all the *search* side of a per-(class x prefix) decomposition needs. The
missing piece is job IDENTITY: two different prefixes very often yield the same
canonical output set and therefore the same bound, and the campaign must
deduplicate jobs by that canonical key. At n=13 the canonicalisation is not
computable outside the engine.

    sortnetopt canon-key <channels> [--prefix-file PATH] [--prefix "a-b,c-d"]

applies each prefix to the full cube exactly as cmd_search does, canonicalises it
exactly as the search memoises its root, and prints
    <prefix>\t<width>\t<popcount>\t<hex canonical packed key>
It does not start the thread pool, the stats thread or a StateMap, so it has none
of search's ~10-second process-wall floor.

The decomposition identity this serves, and its proof, for the record:

    For any depth L and any n, letting X_P be the canonical output set obtained
    by applying a length-L comparator prefix P to the full n-cube and s(X_P) the
    minimum number of comparators sorting X_P,

        S(n)  =  min over all length-L prefixes P of ( L + s(X_P) ).

    (>=) An optimal n-sorter's first L comparators form some P and its tail
    sorts X_P, so S(n) - L >= s(X_P). (<=) P followed by an optimal completion
    of X_P is an n-sorter. Prefixes with equal canonical X_P have equal s, so
    deduplication by canonical key is exact.


================================================================================
EPISTEMIC STATUS OF THE CAMPAIGN THIS SERVES — READ BEFORE USING PART 3
================================================================================

Parts 1 and 2 are unconditional engine machinery. Part 3 is unconditional as
stated above (the prefix decomposition identity is elementary).

What is CONDITIONAL is the *class* restriction that tools/class_filter.py layers
on top of Part 3. The three classes C1 (root split 5|8), C2 (6|7) and C3 (4|9)
are exhaustive over admissible shapes only under the bridge statement E1' /
eq (8) of van Voorhis's two-channel argument. Per docs/van-voorhis-theory-report.md
and docs/kraft-repair-report.md:

  * E1' is confirmed to be the primary source's own statement, and the tree is
    confirmed to be the MAX branch tree rooted at the max output;
  * eq (8) is PROVED only for *clean* sorters (no pass-through comparators on
    any max-path). Pass-throughs are the sole obstruction, and they occur in 27
    of 43 constructed sorters — the common case, not a corner case;
  * the proposed general repair (LEMMA-star and the whole family of per-node
    charging arguments) was REFUTED by an independent adversarial verifier.
    eq (8) now needs a global argument that does not exist.

Therefore: a completed C1 + C2 + C3 exhaustion is a CONDITIONAL result. An
unconditional lower-bound conclusion needs either the Kraft repair or
all-shapes coverage — the latter being available today as the root-split
partition over ALL shapes, which is exhaustive by construction and depends on no
conjecture, at the cost of covering root splits that the case split would have
excluded.

One further correction is baked into the filter and is worth restating here
because it is easy to get wrong: the class filters must be computed on the
BRANCH TREE — the tree whose nodes are the comparators at which two max-paths
MERGE — and not on literal comparator counts along a max-path. Literal depth is
>= branch depth and the two differ in 27 of 43 networks tested; using literal
depth REJECTS VALID NETWORKS and is unsound as a prune (van-voorhis-theory-report
§6.2). Only Filter 0 (max literal delta <= 5, from the one-channel theorem)
is correct with the literal count.

Nowhere in this patch, in the campaign tool, or in the class filter is any
comparator count used as a search target, bound, feature or stopping condition.
